window.docContents[11].resolve({"/Constructions/#section-box-products":{"contents":"The box product of two graphs G and H is the graph on the Cartesian product of\ntheir vertex sets, where a pair of vertices (g₁, h₁) and (g₂, h₂) are adjacent if\neither g₁ = g₂ and H.Adj h₁ h₂ or h₁ = h₂ and G.Adj g₁ g₂. This is written\nG □ H in Mathlib. Its vertex type is the product type V × W, whose elements are\npairs (g, h); for x : V × W the two components are x.1 and x.2 (also written\nx.fst and x.snd). For each vertex h of H, the vertices (g, h) span a copy\nof G in G □ H, the G-fibre at h; similarly each vertex g of G gives a\ncopy of H, the H-fibre at g.\n\nThe box product of P_3 and K_3\n\n#source boxProd\n\n\nAdjacency in the box product of a path and a cycle. (We use n + 2 because\nMathlib's cycleGraph_adj is stated for cycleGraph (n + 2); the more awkward\ncycleGraph_adj' covers every n.)\n\nexample {n : ℕ} {a b c d : Fin (n + 2)} : (P[n + 2] □ C[n + 2]).Adj (a, b) (c, d) ↔\n    ((a.val + 1 = c.val ∨ c.val + 1 = a.val) ∧ b = d) ∨\n        ((b - d = 1 ∨ d - b = 1) ∧ a = c) := by\n  simp [pathGraph_adj, cycleGraph_adj]\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions\u0009Operations on Graphs","header":"3.3.2. Box Products","id":"/Constructions/#section-box-products"},"/Distance/#chapter-distance":{"contents":"namespace SimpleGraph\n\n\nThe distance between two vertices of a graph measures how far apart they are: it is\nthe length of a shortest walk between them. In a connected graph it makes the vertex\nset into a metric space, and many important graph parameters are defined in terms of\nit: the eccentricity of a vertex as well as the diameter and radius of a graph.\nDistance is also closely related to the girth.\n\nIn this chapter we introduce Mathlib's distance edist and its basic properties,\nand show that the distance in the hypercube Q[n] is the Hamming distance. We study\neccentricity, diameter and radius, with vertex-transitive graphs, the hypercube and\nthe Mycielskian as examples. We then focus on girth, which we compute for cycle\ngraphs, complete bipartite graphs, hypercubes and Mycielskians.\n\nCounting the vertices in the distance layers around a vertex gives the Moore bound\nand the cage bound, which relate the number of vertices of a graph to its degrees,\ndiameter and girth. The Petersen graph shows that both bounds can be attained.\nFinally we prove Albertson's theorem: a precolouring of vertices that are pairwise\nfar apart extends to a colouring with one extra colour.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean","header":"11. Distance","id":"/Distance/#chapter-distance"},"/Walks/#section-adding-a-single-edge":{"contents":"We can now prove the heart of the Bondy–Chvátal theorem: if u and v satisfy\nOre's condition IsOrePair, then G ⊔ edge u v is Hamiltonian iff G is.\n\nThe interesting direction takes a Hamiltonian cycle c of G ⊔ edge u v. If c\navoids the edge u — v then it transfers directly to G. Otherwise our previous\nlemma cuts c open into a Hamiltonian path from u to v avoiding u — v, which\ntransfers to G; since the path is Hamiltonian, every neighbour of u and of v\ntrivially lies on it, so IsHamiltonian.exists_isHamiltonianCycle_of_isOrePair (via\nIsMaximalPath.exists_cycle_spanning) closes it back up into a cycle containing all\nof V, using a crossing pair if u and v are non-adjacent in G. This is where\nthe degree condition enters. The reverse direction is simply monotonicity of\nHamiltonicity (IsHamiltonian.mono in Mathlib).\n\nIn both cases we transfer a walk in G ⊔ edge u v that avoids the new edge u — v\nto G, so we first record that every edge of such a walk is an edge of G.\n\nlemma Walk.edges_mem_edgeSet_of_notMem {u v a b : V} (p : (G ⊔ edge u v).Walk a b)\n    (hp : s(u, v) ∉ p.edges) : ∀ e ∈ p.edges, e ∈ G.edgeSet := fun e he => by\n  have := p.edges_subset_edgeSet he\n  grind [edgeSet_sup, edgeSet_edge_subset]\n\n\ntheorem isHamiltonian_sup_edge_iff [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] {u v : V}\n    [Fintype (G.neighborSet u)] [Fintype (G.neighborSet v)]\n    (hcard : G.IsOrePair u v) :\n    (G ⊔ edge u v).IsHamiltonian ↔ G.IsHamiltonian := by\n  refine ⟨fun hG => ?_, fun hG => hG.mono le_sup_left⟩\n  -- dispose of the degenerate cases, where no edge has been added\n  by_cases hne : u = v\n  · rwa [hne, sup_edge_self] at hG\n  by_cases hadj : G.Adj u v\n  · rwa [G.sup_edge_of_adj hadj] at hG\n  intro h1\n  obtain ⟨a, c, hc⟩ := hG h1\n  -- the graph has at least three vertices, since it has a cycle\n  have h3 : 3 ≤ Fintype.card V := hc.length_eq ▸ hc.isCycle.three_le_length\n  by_cases hmem : s(u, v) ∈ c.edges\n  · -- the cycle uses the new edge: cut it open into a Hamiltonian path\n    obtain ⟨p, hham, hpe⟩ := hc.exists_isHamiltonian_walk_of_mem_edges hmem\n    -- the path avoids the new edge, so it transfers to G, where we close it up\n    have hq := hham.transfer (p.edges_mem_edgeSet_of_notMem hpe)\n    exact ⟨u, hq.exists_isHamiltonianCycle_of_isOrePair (by grind [hq.length_eq])\n      hcard⟩\n  · -- the cycle avoids the new edge: transfer it to G directly\n    exact ⟨a, _, hc.transfer (c.edges_mem_edgeSet_of_notMem hmem)⟩\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks\u0009Hamiltonian Graphs: Ore, Dirac and Bondy–Chvátal*\u0009The Bondy–Chvátal Theorem","header":"8.8.4.1. Adding a Single Edge","id":"/Walks/#section-adding-a-single-edge"},"/Walks/#section-vertices-and-edges-of-a-walk":{"contents":"A walk visits a sequence of vertices and traverses a sequence of edges, and Mathlib\nrecords both as lists.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks","header":"8.2. Vertices and Edges of a Walk","id":"/Walks/#section-vertices-and-edges-of-a-walk"}});